# CI/CD Recovery Pipeline Demo

This repository demonstrates a local CI/CD pipeline for an online payment
service. It starts production on a stable `v1`, validates a new `v2`, deploys it
through staging, intentionally fails the production health check, and then
rolls production back to `v1`.

The current implementation is Phase 2 of the demo progression: a repeatable
local shell-script pipeline with reset and inspection helpers.

## Scenario

Traditional CI/CD flow:

```text
build -> test -> security -> staging deploy -> staging health
      -> production deploy -> production health -> rollback on failure
```

In this repo:

- `v1` is the previous known-good production version.
- `v2` is the new version being delivered.
- `v2` passes build, test, security, staging deploy, and staging health.
- A production health failure is intentionally injected.
- `rollback.sh production` restores production to `v1`.

## Main command

Run the full local scenario:

```sh
./scripts/pipeline.sh
```

Expected final result:

- staging has received `v2`;
- production briefly receives `v2`;
- production health fails because failure was injected;
- rollback restores production to `v1`;
- the final state output shows production back on `v1`.

## Useful commands

Reset the local demo state:

```sh
./scripts/reset.sh
```

Show the current local state:

```sh
./scripts/show-state.sh
```

Inject the simulated production health failure:

```sh
./scripts/inject-failure.sh production
```

Run individual stages:

```sh
./scripts/build.sh v2
./scripts/run-tests.sh v2
./scripts/security-scan.sh v2
./scripts/deploy.sh staging v2
./scripts/health-check.sh staging
./scripts/deploy.sh production v2
./scripts/health-check.sh production
./scripts/rollback.sh production
```

## Script roles

| Script | Role |
| --- | --- |
| `scripts/reset.sh` | Restores the local demo to a clean baseline with production on `v1` |
| `scripts/pipeline.sh` | Orchestrates the full CI/CD and rollback scenario |
| `scripts/build.sh` | Validates the selected app version has required files and version metadata |
| `scripts/run-tests.sh` | Runs simple service checks for the selected version |
| `scripts/security-scan.sh` | Scans the selected version for simple hardcoded secret patterns |
| `scripts/deploy.sh` | Copies a version into staging or production deployment folders |
| `scripts/health-check.sh` | Checks deployed health and fails production when the failure marker exists |
| `scripts/inject-failure.sh` | Creates the marker file that forces production health to fail |
| `scripts/rollback.sh` | Restores production to the previous known-good version |
| `scripts/show-state.sh` | Prints the current versions and failure-injection state |

## Failure injection

The production failure is deliberate. `scripts/inject-failure.sh production`
creates:

```text
state/force_production_health_failure
```

When `scripts/health-check.sh production` sees that file, it exits with failure.
This proves the pipeline can detect a bad production health check and trigger the
predefined rollback.

## Current limitations

This phase is still a local simulation:

- the service is represented by static files under `app/v1` and `app/v2`;
- deployments are file copies into `deployments/staging` and
  `deployments/production`;
- health checks read local files instead of calling an HTTP API.

Later phases can replace this with a real payment-service API, real tests,
Docker containers for staging and production, and then GitLab CI or GitHub
Actions.
